The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ret)
    return ERR_PTR(ret);
```
(occurs twice: after `sg_alloc_table_from_pages` and after `dma_map_sgtable`)

Reason:
When `sg_alloc_table_from_pages` or `dma_map_sgtable` fails, the function directly returns an error pointer without freeing the previously allocated `sg` (via `kzalloc`) or the internal scatter-gather table memory (via `sg_alloc_table_from_pages`). This causes a memory leak because the allocated resources are not cleaned up on the error path.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ret)
    goto err_free_sg;
```
and
```c
if (ret)
    goto err_free_table;
```
along with the added cleanup labels:
```c
err_free_table:
    sg_free_table(sg);
err_free_sg:
    kfree(sg);
    return ERR_PTR(ret);
```

Reason:
The fix replaces the direct `return ERR_PTR(ret)` with `goto` statements that jump to appropriate cleanup labels. If `sg_alloc_table_from_pages` fails, it jumps to `err_free_sg` to free the `sg` structure allocated by `kzalloc`. If `dma_map_sgtable` fails, it jumps to `err_free_table` which first frees the scatter-gather table (via `sg_free_table`) and then falls through to `err_free_sg` to free the `sg` structure. This ensures all allocated resources are properly freed before returning the error.

